{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "4 - Clearance: the UI, tests, and your first change",
  "description": "Learn the optional React/TypeScript console, form-to-wire conversion, state, polling cleanup, privacy boundaries, test evidence, and a practical change exercise. Builds on chapters 1-3; no prior React knowledge assumed.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/main.tsx",
      "pattern": "^const container = document\\.getElementById\\(\"root\"\\);",
      "title": "1. See how browser code starts",
      "description": "**Prerequisite:** you can now follow the backend from chapters 1-3. The frontend observes that system; it is not where money becomes durable. This file finds the HTML element `root`, fails explicitly if missing, and renders `<App />` into it. `frontend/package.json` defines Vite's dev command, Vitest tests, and a build that runs TypeScript before Vite.\n\nReact builds a UI from **components**, functions describing what to show from current inputs/state. **JSX** is the HTML-like syntax in `.tsx` files; the build transforms it into JavaScript. `<App />` calls upon a component, not a Go service. Imports connect modules and also load styles.\n\n`StrictMode` enables extra development checks, including extra effect setup/cleanup cycles. It is not a security boundary, and that development behavior is not a promise of duplicate production requests. See [React's effect lifecycle](https://react.dev/learn/synchronizing-with-effects).\n\nBenefit: a component tree divides presentation into understandable pieces. Cost: learning render/state/effect lifecycles and a build toolchain. A plain HTML form would be simpler for a single submission, but less convenient for this interactive receipt console.\n\n**Check yourself:** Does displaying a PENDING receipt prove the backend lost it if the tab is refreshed?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. Browser state can disappear while the durable PostgreSQL transaction remains. The backend and UI have separate lifetimes."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/types.ts",
      "pattern": "^export type TransactionStatus =",
      "title": "2. Read TypeScript without overtrusting it",
      "description": "`type TransactionStatus = \"PENDING\" | \"AUTHORIZED\" | \"FAILED\"` is a union of allowed string literals. `interface TransactionPayload` describes the four request-body fields. In the separate `TransactionResponse` interface, `correlation_id?: string` means that response field may be absent. `Receipt[]` means an array of receipts. `import type` imports information for type checking rather than a runtime value.\n\nFollow three separate shapes: `TransactionInput` stores form text in camelCase, `TransactionPayload` sends snake_case fields and a numeric amount, and `Receipt` adds UI information such as preview and creation time. Keeping them distinct exposes conversions instead of quietly treating every object as interchangeable.\n\nTypeScript checks code before execution; external JSON still needs runtime validation. In `frontend/src/lib/api.ts`, `request<T>` is a generic function: callers supply an expected result type `T`, and `Promise<T>` describes its eventual result. `response.json() as Promise<T>` is an assertion to the compiler, not a check of the server's bytes. Likewise, `as TransactionStatus` in the hook cannot turn an invalid string into a valid status.\n\nBenefit: typed contracts catch many development mistakes. Cost: duplicated frontend/backend schemas can drift, and unchecked assertions leave runtime trust gaps.\n\n**Check yourself:** If the server returns status='SURPRISE', will the union type reject the JSON at runtime?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. The current assertion does not validate it. A runtime validator would need explicit checks; its absence is a current limitation, not an implemented feature."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/lib/transaction.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function buildTransactionPayload\\(",
      "title": "3. Convert form text at the boundary",
      "description": "Browser input arrives as text. `String(...).trim()` removes surrounding whitespace; currency becomes uppercase; `/^\\d+$/` requires the amount text to contain only digits. `Number(amountText)` converts it, then `Number.isSafeInteger` and `> 0` reject unsafe or invalid amounts. JavaScript's number type represents large integers less broadly than Go's `int64`; the safe-integer check matters.\n\nThe returned object deliberately has exactly `account_id`, `merchant_id`, `amount_cents`, and `currency`. Extra keys can fail the Go decoder's `DisallowUnknownFields` policy. `buildTransactionHeaders` separately sets bearer, idempotency, and correlation headers. This is a contract between processes, not simply renaming variables for style.\n\nBenefit: immediate form feedback avoids unnecessary requests. Cost: matching validation exists on both sides and must be kept consistent. The server still validates because clients can bypass this UI. Three letters only establish currency syntax, and reserved account IDs are still a backend rule.\n\n`frontend/test/lib/transaction.test.ts` checks payloads and headers. Compare these checks to `internal/transaction/service.go` instead of assuming they are identical.\n\n**Check yourself:** What should typing 125.50 into the amount-cents field do? What should be typed for $125.50 USD?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** It is rejected because the field expects whole cents. Type 12550. This conversion does not multiply a decimal dollar input by 100."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/lib/api.ts",
      "pattern": "^async function request<T>\\(",
      "title": "4. Distinguish network failures from HTTP failures",
      "description": "`await fetch(...)` waits for an HTTP response. A network failure rejects the Promise and enters `catch`; an HTTP 400 or 500 still produces a response, so `response.ok` must be checked separately. `parseApiError` reads an error message when possible and falls back to the HTTP status when the response isn't usable JSON.\n\n`submitTransaction({ baseUrl, authValue, ... }: SubmitArgs)` uses object **destructuring** to unpack named inputs. Backticks with `${...}` create a template string. `JSON.stringify(payload)` serializes the request body. `getTransaction` URL-encodes the transaction ID and includes the read credential.\n\nBenefit: one helper gives submissions and reads consistent error handling. Cost: a shared helper also shares limitations: it asserts success-body types and does not itself configure a timeout or AbortController cancellation. A rejected request doesn't always prove the server never committed; keep the idempotency key when retrying that uncertain operation.\n\n`frontend/test/lib/api.test.ts` uses a fake fetch for the authenticated GET contract and checks error parsing. It does not exercise a real network or the whole submission UI.\n\n**Check yourself:** Why doesn't a try/catch around fetch alone catch a 409 conflict? Is a network error proof that nothing was created?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** fetch resolves on HTTP error responses, so inspect response.ok. A network error may occur after the backend accepted work; retry with the same logical operation key."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/App.tsx",
      "pattern": "^export function App\\(\\)",
      "title": "5. Trace props, local state, and rendered output",
      "description": "`const { state, actions } = useConsole()` unpacks the hook's result. A **hook** is a React function for state/lifecycle behavior; this custom hook coordinates console operations. `const [settingsOpen, setSettingsOpen] = useState(false)` stores a value across renders and returns a setter that schedules its update.\n\nComponents receive **props**, ordinary inputs such as `receipts={state.receipts}`. Curly braces evaluate JavaScript inside JSX. `settingsOpen && (...)` conditionally displays settings. `state.receipts[0] ?? null` uses null only when there is no first value. `<>...</>` groups elements without adding a wrapper node.\n\n`onToggleSettings={() => setSettingsOpen((open) => !open)}` passes a callback; it does not call it during rendering. The inner updater derives the next state from the current value. `useMemo` caches the computed summary while its dependencies remain equal; it doesn't fetch server truth.\n\nBenefit: presentation components receive small explicit inputs. Cost: you must trace props and state ownership across files. The skip link and semantic main element help keyboard navigation, but their presence alone does not prove the whole UI is accessible.\n\n**Check yourself:** Does the number in MetricStrip represent every transaction in the database?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. It summarizes the in-memory receipt list passed through App, currently capped at twelve. Browser summaries are not backend financial reports."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/state/useConsole.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function useConsole\\(",
      "title": "6. Read state updates as transitions",
      "description": "`useState(init)` creates console state using `init` and returns a `setState` function. An update such as `setState((current) => ({ ...current, submitting: true, notice: null }))` receives the latest state and returns the next state. The arrow function is a callback; spreading `current` preserves the fields that this update does not change.\n\n`[receipt, ...current.receipts].slice(0, MAX_RECEIPTS)` prepends a receipt and keeps twelve. `.map(...)` replaces only matching receipt objects after a status read. These copies preserve the old state and make updates visible through changed object references.\n\nBenefit: direct state updates keep this small console easy to follow. Cost: related transitions are spread across submission, polling, and notice callbacks, so read all paths when changing a shared field.\n\nSubmission uses try/catch/finally: success adds a receipt, failure shows a notice, and finally clears 'submitting' either way. Generating a new idempotency key starts a new logical operation; keeping the old one supports a retry. The local receipt list is not deduplicated into a server history.\n\n**Check yourself:** If an HTTP retry returns the same transaction ID, does adding another receipt imply the backend charged twice?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. Local receipt creation and backend idempotency are different layers. Compare transaction IDs and durable ledger effects before drawing that conclusion."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/state/useConsole.ts",
      "pattern": "^  useEffect\\(\\(\\) => \\{",
      "title": "7. Explain polling, closures, and cleanup",
      "description": "`useEffect` synchronizes with an external system after rendering. Here it captures pending receipt IDs and immediately fetches their current status, then repeats every 1,500 ms. `Promise.all` waits for the group of reads; a `polling` flag prevents overlap within this effect instance. Completed statuses update the matching receipt with setState.\n\nThe dependency array contains API URL, auth value, and receipts. When dependencies change, React runs the old cleanup before setting up again. Each setup has its own captured variables, a **closure**. Cleanup sets `stopped = true` and clears the interval; old results then skip state updates. This suppresses stale updates but does not abort existing requests or guarantee no network overlap across effect instances. See [React useEffect](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect).\n\nBenefit: polling works with ordinary HTTP and the durable GET route. Costs: delay, traffic, and rate-limit pressure. Twelve pending receipts at roughly forty polls per minute can request about 480 reads per minute before latency and reruns, well above the default shared limit of 60. It silently retries read failures, so PENDING can hide an auth/rate-limit problem.\n\nWebSockets or server-sent events could reduce polling traffic, but would add connection/reconnect management. No such push path is present here. frontend/test/useConsole.test.ts covers a failed read followed by successful polling, and checks that polling stops after a final status; it does not prove every cleanup or stale-response scenario.\n\n**Check yourself:** Why both clearInterval and stopped? Does void refresh() mean the reads run synchronously?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** clearInterval stops future timer ticks; stopped suppresses a result already in flight. void discards the returned Promise value; the asynchronous function still runs."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/state/useConsole.ts",
      "pattern": "^function init\\(",
      "title": "8. Separate demo configuration from durable state",
      "description": "`init` reads the API URL from `VITE_API_BASE_URL`, trims whitespace and trailing slashes, and uses the local Go service default for blank input. The demo bearer comes from `VITE_TRANSACTION_API_AUTH_VALUE` only in development. This console does not persist its URL, bearer, or receipts in localStorage.\n\nBenefit: startup configuration removes the connection-settings UI. Cost: the destination must be configured before starting the frontend, and reload clears the visible receipt history. Vite environment values are frontend configuration, not a secret vault; code running in the page can read the development credential. Choose a trusted API destination before sending it.\n\n`frontend/test/useConsole.test.ts` checks URL defaults and normalization, submission failures, status polling, receipt limits, and the production credential guard. The server's GET route is the source for durable status. A browser receipt cannot reconstruct the entire ledger, and its risk preview cannot establish sufficient funds.\n\nThis distinction is useful beyond this repo: identify who owns each piece of state, how long it lives, and what happens when its owner restarts.\n\n**Check yourself:** After reload, which data is restored by configuration and which data disappears from this tab?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** The configured URL and development bearer are read again; the receipt list starts empty. The backend's business records survive independently in PostgreSQL."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/transaction/service_test.go",
      "pattern": "^func TestServiceReplaysSameIdempotencyKeyAndRejectsPayloadMismatch\\(",
      "title": "9. Read assertions as evidence",
      "description": "A Go test has a `Test...` name and a `*testing.T` parameter. Setup constructs inputs/dependencies; the call exercises behavior; assertions fail when results are wrong. `t.Fatalf` reports a failure and stops this test's current execution. `t.Parallel()` allows concurrent tests, so each needs isolated state.\n\nRead this test's concrete claims: two identical requests return the same transaction ID; there remains one outbox event; changing the amount under the same key yields `ErrIdempotencyConflict`, recognized by `errors.Is`. `NewMemoryStore()` makes this fast and deterministic without infrastructure.\n\nThat **unit test** checks the service against a fake. It does not prove a PostgreSQL unique constraint, transaction rollback, or two real connections racing. Even the neighboring concurrent-insert test simulates its race with a custom fake. To evaluate a test, ask which real boundary it crosses and which behavior it replaces.\n\nOptional local Go check from the repo root, if Go 1.25 is installed: `rtk go test ./internal/domain ./internal/transaction`. Expected: both packages pass. The Makefile also provides Docker-based Go runners; reading this tour never requires starting services.\n\n**Check yourself:** Which assertion would catch accidentally creating an event again on an identical retry?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** The outbox event count remaining one. Equal transaction IDs alone wouldn't prove that no duplicate downstream work was created."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/store_integration_test.go",
      "pattern": "^func TestProcessRiskEvaluatedRollsBackStateWhenOutboxInsertFails\\(",
      "title": "10. Prove the database boundary with a real failure",
      "description": "This **integration test** uses PostgreSQL and installs a trigger that rejects the final outcome's outbox insert. It then processes risk and expects an error. The assertions check that status remains PENDING, payment ledger rows remain zero, and the incoming processed-event record remains absent. Together those checks show the ledger-stage database transaction rolled back its related writes for this failure.\n\n`TestSaveConsumerOutboxIsIdempotentAndAtomic` in the same file checks duplicate/conflicting deliveries and rollback of a risk-stage claim when its outbox insertion fails. The funding/query integration test checks balanced funding, idempotency, audit, event, and query behavior. Tests establish specific properties, not a universal correctness certificate.\n\n**Execution boundary:** the helper executes `drop schema public cascade; create schema public`. Run integration-tagged tests only with `CLEARANCE_TEST_DATABASE_URL` pointing to a disposable test database. Without the variable, tests skip; that is not a passing integration proof. Inspect `.github/workflows/ci.yml` for the isolated CI database setup.\n\nCurrent gaps include a dedicated real concurrent-spend regression, an actual UPDATE/DELETE immutability test, initial-creation rollback fault injection, and the combined offset/DLQ failure from chapter 3.\n\n**Check yourself:** If only the 'returned an error' assertion existed, would rollback be proved?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. The code could return an error after partially writing state. Checking the database's post-failure state is what makes the test meaningful."
    },
    {
      "file": ".github/workflows/ci.yml",
      "pattern": "^jobs:",
      "title": "11. Choose the verification layer that matches the claim",
      "description": "CI is executable documentation of expected checks. Go unit tests, PostgreSQL integration tests, vet, formatting, and builds verify different things. Frontend Vitest tests exercise helper behavior; TypeScript checks type consistency; a Vite build checks bundling. None alone proves a browser journey or broker outage recovery.\n\nFor the existing frontend dependencies, from `frontend/` use `rtk npm test` and `rtk npm run typecheck`. Expected: helper tests and type checking pass. These commands do not start the backend or prove that a payment completes.\n\nRead `scripts/broker-failure-suite.sh` as a system-level experiment: fund an account, authorize, republish an original event and check unchanged effects, stop Redpanda until outbox work is dead-lettered, restart/requeue, and inject malformed input to inspect the durable DLQ. It creates an isolated Compose project and cleans its own volumes; inspect that isolation before executing it. This tour does not automatically run operational commands.\n\nA **build** proves code can become an executable; a **test** checks selected behavior; **lint/vet** flag suspicious code. 'CI contains a job' is not evidence of its latest run's result.\n\n**Check yourself:** Can green helper tests and type checking prove polling cleans up correctly when the API URL changes?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. That needs a lifecycle/component test or a browser test exercising the change and stale result behavior. Use the test layer matching the claim."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/domain/risk_test.go",
      "pattern": "^func TestEvaluateRiskUsesSimpleAmountRule\\(",
      "title": "12. Finish with a concrete change and a debugging exercise",
      "description": "**Paper exercise first:** suppose the requested risk threshold changes to 75,000 cents. Identify the backend rule and reason text in `internal/domain/domain.go`, the frontend `RISK_THRESHOLD_CENTS` in `frontend/src/lib/constants.ts`, and the preview in `frontend/src/lib/risk.ts` that imports it and derives its reason text. Check the corresponding tests in `internal/domain/risk_test.go` and `frontend/test/lib/risk.test.ts`. Explain why changing only the preview would mislead users. No schema change is needed merely to change this rule.\n\nPredict boundary results: 74,999 LOW, 75,000 LOW, 75,001 HIGH. Then design a test showing that a LOW-risk payment can still fail for insufficient funds. Backend policy, UI preview, and available balance are three separate concerns. If you implement this practice later, keep it on a practice branch and include all affected expectations.\n\n**Debugging exercise:** after a 202 receipt stays PENDING, choose evidence in order: GET durable status; inspect creation/outbox state; inspect publication/consumer failures; inspect risk/ledger effects; inspect UI polling/auth if the database is already final. Don't begin by changing the risk threshold or generating a fresh idempotency key.\n\nYou are ready to make a small change when you can point to its owner, explain its syntax, name a plausible alternative and cost, predict its failure case, and choose the test that would catch a regression. Revisit the relevant chapter when one of those answers is missing.\n\n**Check yourself:** Give two plausible causes of PENDING that require changes in different layers.\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** Backend: an outbox event exhausted publishing attempts, so processing stalled. Frontend: the database finalized but authenticated polling keeps failing or is rate-limited. Inspect the durable row to distinguish them."
    }
  ]
}
